-- title:
--   Representability of the H₁=Γ₀(M')∩Γ₁(ℓ) Weierstrass problem
-- statement:
--   Let $A$ be a Noetherian commutative ring, let $q$ and $\ell$ be primes with $\ell\ge 5$, and let $M'$ be a nonzero natural number, with $\ell$ and $M'$ units in $A$. Assume the three equivariance rules for the level data under Weierstrass changes of variables $C$ over any $A$-algebra $T$: that `IsGamma1Point` for $\ell$ (the conditions that $(x_P,y_P)$ satisfies the affine equation, that $\mathrm{pre}\Psi_\ell(x_P)=0$, and that $(x_Q,y_Q)=(x_P,y_P)$) is preserved by the induced action on `LevelPData` (hypothesis `hℓ`); that `IsGamma0PowAt` at $p^k$ — the two-torsion condition `IsTwoKernel` when $p^k=2$, otherwise `IsCyclicGenKernel` (degree at most $\varphi(p^k)/2$, coefficient $1$ in that degree, $h\cdot\mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^{k-1}}\mid \mathrm{pre}\Psi_{p^k}$, and $h$ dividing the relevant multiplication numerators) — is preserved by `kernelVariableChangeDeg` (hypothesis `hM`); and that divisibility of `inLineMulPoly W ℓ n x` by $h$ is preserved, with $h$ replaced by `kernelVariableChangeDeg C d h` and $x$ by $u^{-2}(x-r)$ (hypothesis `hL`). Let $\mathcal G$ be a family of relative group laws on the graded projective models of unit-discriminant Weierstrass curves over $A$-algebras, assumed chord–tangent (each law is realised by an addition-and-Galois-compatible identification of its points over fields with the affine point group) and with identity at the origin (the unit section is cut out by a ring map from the origin chart killing $x/y$ and $z/y$). Let $\mathcal T$ be a level transport for $\mathcal G$ at $q$, functorial in $A$-algebra maps and in variable changes and preserving the property that a raw pair has the given curve and that its two sections form a Drinfeld basis of level $q$; assume $\mathcal T$ is a section transport, i.e. its structural maps agree with the geometric ones after composing with any graded homomorphism realising the variable change, respectively the coefficient change. Assume finally that such graded homomorphisms exist: for every variable change (`hVC`) and every $A$-algebra map (`hCO`) there is a graded ring homomorphism between the projective-model graded rings, carrying the irrelevant ideal appropriately and satisfying `IsVariableChangeHom`, respectively `IsCoefficientHom`. Then the level moduli datum attached to `rigidDataH1Pow A ℓ M' q hℓ hM hL 𝒢 𝒯` — whose objects are unit-discriminant Weierstrass curves over $A$-algebras equipped with a tuple of generator-kernel polynomials indexed by the prime factors of $M'$, an $\ell$-point, and a raw Drinfeld pair of level $q$, subject to the link condition that when $\ell\mid M'$ the $\ell$-component divides $\mathrm{inLineMulPoly}$ at $\ell^{v_\ell(M')-1}$ and $x_P$, all taken up to variable change — admits an abstract representing package $P_0$: a commutative $A$-algebra $B_0$ with a universal point, such that $B_0$ is of finite type over $A$ and integral over the $A$-subalgebra of $B_0$ generated by the single element `P₀.j₀`, the $j$-invariant of the universal point.
--
--   This is the representability and finiteness-over-the-$j$-line statement for the Weierstrass moduli problem with $H_1=\Gamma_0(M')\cap\Gamma_1(\ell)$ level together with an auxiliary Drinfeld $\Gamma(q)$-structure, in the form in which the auxiliary rigidifying level is a $\Gamma_1(\ell)$-point linked to the $\Gamma_0(M')$-tuple rather than a full $\Gamma(\ell)$-structure. It supplies the coarse ring $B_0$ over which the later analysis of level automorphisms, completions at supersingular points and Tate-curve expansions is carried out.
